            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:

** The old bathroom exhaust fan. It looks like the exhaust fan damper is not sealing well and could be replaced with the new ENERGY STAR* exhaust fan with butterfly damper.

** From the floor beside the hot water tank. This is a significant area of air leakage into the main floor of your home, bringing cool air from the crawlspace with it. This could be sealed up with spray foam application.

** The top of the old windows frames. It appears pink fiberglass insulation installed inside the frames of the old windows, which is a good insulator but a poor air sealer. Removing the fiberglass insulation and spraying foam insulation will seal the windows good.

** There?????????s quite a bit of air infiltration getting into the floor, and ultimately coming through the vents. A continuous air barrier should be resolved in the floor

** The rear patio door is not sealing properly due to a broken latch. Installing a new latch will significantly reduce the air leakage from that door.

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 30% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 370 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 15 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year

</airSeal>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house except the one in the office room are older double pane vinyl windows, that are in need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $80 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 189 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 1,150 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.6 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 82.8% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing oil furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 750 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

** During the on-site investigation we noticed furnace ducting exposed to the cold crawlspace air. I'd recommend to seal the exposed furnace ducting with weather resistant aluminum foil tape and it should be wrapped in insulation.

** Your house have a 6" combustion air intake with non-motorized damper, which means it is allowing cold outside air even when the furnace is off. One good option is to install a motorized damper, when the thermostat calls for heat, this motorized damper opens to allow fresh air for complete combustion. When the furnace stops, the damper closes to prevent the entry of unnecessary cold air.   

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on an exhaust equipment (bathroom fan), indicated a pressure drop of 0.5 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - one bathroom fan



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



